A Heap is a complete binary tree data structure that satisfies the heap property: 
in a min-heap, the value of each child is greater than or equal to its parent, 
and in a max-heap, the value of each child is less than or equal to its parent.
 Heaps are commonly used to implement priority queues, where the smallest (or largest) 
 element is always at the root.


 So, in a HEAP, we can actually arrange them inside like an array. Like, for a binay heap tree,
 if we go level-wise, then we can easily put them inside an array. Now, if there is a NODE at
 index i, then the left child is at (2*i) and the right child is at (2*i+1) and its parent node is
 at floor of (i/2).

